WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
internet service providers were not willy nilly blocking traffic and throttling traffic.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.560
To the contrary, these were all phantoms that were conjured up

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.460
by people who wanted the FCC for political reasons to over-regulate the internet.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.020
Hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason and today we are talking with Ajit Pai.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.720
He's the chairman of the Federal Communications Commission,

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.300
which oversees the licensing of radio and television stations,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.440
creates ownership roles for certain types of media companies,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.620
polices broadcast radio and television for indecency,

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.180
and over the past few years has tried to enforce controversial rules

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.600
that will maintain a free and open internet, sometimes called net neutrality.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.000
Ajit, thanks for talking to us.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.320
Nick, great to be with you again.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.500
You are repealing Title II rules,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.340
explain what that will do and what you hope to accomplish with that.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.260
Well, as you pointed out, Title II involves the panoply of heavy-handed economic regulations

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.640
that were developed in the Great Depression to handle Ma Bell, the telephone monopoly of the 1930s.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.500
My previous colleagues imposed those rules on the internet,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.420
one of the most dynamic systems we've ever known.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.720
Earlier I proposed to my fellow commissioners at the FCC

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.500
to repeal those Title II regulations.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.420
Going forward, my hope is that in a more free market, light touch environment,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.860
we can figure out what the right regulatory framework is

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.720
to preserve those core protections of a free and open internet

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:31.080
that have existed prior to 2015 when on a party-line vote, the FCC adopted these net neutrality regulations.

